            "pent6;polygon;pentagon;A,VcA,c,Vcd,VAB;0;0;black",
            "pent7;polygon;pentagon;a,VaC,C,VCD,Vab;0;0;black",
            "pent8;polygon;pentagon;b,VbD,D,VCD,Vab;0;0;black",
            "pent9;polygon;pentagon;b,Vab,a,Vad,Vbc;0;0;black",
            "pent10;polygon;pentagon;b,Vbc,c,VcA,VbD;0;0;black",
            "pent11;polygon;pentagon;c,Vcd,d,Vad,Vbc;0;0;black",
            "pent12;polygon;pentagon;a,Vad,d,VdB,VaC;0;0;black",
            "m1;point;midpoint;VcA,VdB;0;0",
            "m2;point;midpoint;VAB,VCD;0;0",
            "m3;point;midpoint;Vad,VBC;0;0",
            "rect1;polygon;parallelogram;Vbc,Vad,m3;0;0;blue;210,30,100",
            "rect6;polygon;parallelogram;VbD,VcA,m1;0;0;blue;160,30,100",
            "rect3;polygon;parallelogram;Vab,VCD,m2;0;0;blue;160,30,100",
            "rect2;polygon;parallelogram;VaC,VdB,m1;0;0;blue;160,30,100",
            "rect5;polygon;parallelogram;VAD,VBC,m3;0;0;blue;210,30,100",
            "rect4;polygon;parallelogram;Vcd,VAB,m2;0;0;blue;160,30,100"
        ],
    });
</script>
</figure>

The points *R, S,* and *T* cut the lines they're on into extreme and mean ratios, so they have these coordinates:

<center><i>R</i> = (–<i>x,</i>1,0), <i>S</i> = (<i>x,</i>1,0), <i>T</i> = (0,<i>x,</i>–1),</center>

where *x* equals (√5 – 1)/2. Finally, points *U, V,* and *W* are outside the original cube with the coordinates

<center><i>U</i> = (–<i>x,</i>1+<i>x,</i>0), <i>V</i> = (<i>x,</i>1+<i>x,</i>0), <i>W</i> = (0,<i>x,</i>–1–<i>x</i>),</center>

Besides the 8 vertices of the cube in the dodecahedron, there are 12 more vertices. These occur in three groups of 4, each group being the vertices of a rectangle. Each rectangle lies on one of the planes passing through the center of the dodecahedron and parallel to one of the faces of the cube.

Using the coordinate system mentioned above, the four corners of the rectangle in the *xy*-plane have coordinates (±*x*, ±(1+*x*), 0), where the pluses and minuses are taken on all combinations, and *x* equals (√5 – 1)/2.

#### Use of this construction

Constructing a dodecahedron is an end in itself. This construction and the corollary are also used in @XIII.18 where the five regular polyhedra are compared.
